'''Hit delay''' is measured in [[RuneScape clock|game ticks]] and is the amount of time an attack will be queued to an entity ([[Player character|player]] or [[NPCs|NPC]]) without doing damage. Specifically, hit delay measures the amount of ticks an attack is processed on an entity without damaging it. Melee attacks damage an entity the very first tick that they are processed, and as such, are defined to have a hit delay of 0.

The hit delay of distance attacks is typically variable based on the {{wp|Chebyshev distance}} between the entity dealing the attack and the entity receiving the attack. The distance is typically measured edge-to-edge in [[game square]]s, using the same edge for both entities. I.e. distance will be calculated using an NPC's closest edge to the player, and the player's furthest edge from the [[NPC]]. However, [[barrage spells]] and [[thralls]] are notable exceptions in that they calculate distance from the attacker to the defender's south-west tile, which causes abnormally long hit delay when attacking a large NPC from the north or east. 

==Processing order delay==
Attacks will hit on different ticks depending on whether the attacking or defending entity is processed first in a tick. If the entity receiving the hit is processed earlier than the entity dealing the hit, then the hit will be delayed by an additional one tick.

[[NPCs]] are processed earlier than players each tick, so this effect will make all hits on [[NPCs]] delayed by an additional one tick compared to the numbers listed in this article.

In the case of [[PvP]], [[PID]] will be the determining factor whether or not processing order delay will delay attacks by one tick.

==Melee==
When attacking using a melee weapon, with few exceptions such as the [[Dragon_claws#Special_attack|dragon claws special attack]], the hit delay is 0 game ticks.<br>This also applies to [[salamander]]s, even when using their Flare (Ranged) and Blaze (Magic) attack styles.

